  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Strong low pressure system will bring widespread rainfall and\ngusty winds to the area on Thanksgiving Day.\n\nHeading into Wednesday, a complex upper level pattern will lead \nto unsettled weather through the Thanksgiving holiday across the\nregion. Two upper level shortwave features, with one across the\nGulf states, and another across the central U.S. will phase,\nresulting the rapid development of a deepening low pressure \nsystem that will move up the east coast through Friday.\n\nStill some differences among global models on the interaction of\nthe two waves which in turn effect the track of the low and also\nthe timing and location of the heaviest rainfall. The ECMWF\ncontinues to fully phase both waves and consolidates into a\nvertically stacked closed low, slowing the forward progression\nof the system. On the other hand, the GFS keeps more separation\nbetween the two pieces and this allows for a more progressive\nmovement of the low. With the more progressive scenario, this\nhas the bulk of the rainfall done by mid-afternoon on \nThanksgiving Day, whereas the slower evolution brings the\nheaviest rainfall in during the afternoon on Thanksgiving.\nAt this time, it appears the GFS is the most progressive even\ncompared to the AIGFS/EC-AIFS suites as well as the Canadian. So\nfor now, will favor a slightly slower low progression with the\nheaviest rain falling through midday Thursday.\n\nAlthough timing differences still exist, all models give the\nforecast area a widespread, beneficial rainfall. Anywhere from 1\nto 2 inches is becoming more likely with perhaps localized \nhigher amounts depending on where the axis of the heaviest rain \nsets up. Highest amounts favor locations east of the Blue Ridge. \nAny flooding potential seems limited given low stream levels and\nongoing drought conditions. However, freshly fallen leaves \ncould pose a urban flooding threat due to clogged drains and \nculverts.\n\nA secondary threat appears to be gusty winds across the region\nas a result of the rapidly deepening low. A very impressive\n850mb jet nearing 70kts will move overhead on Thursday, which\nwill likely translate to gusty easterly winds throughout much \nof the day. However, the biggest uncertainty here will be how \nmuch of the wind can mix to the surface given a stout wedge in \nplace that coincides with the strongest low-level jet passage. \nThe stable wedge may prevent the strongest winds from reaching \nthe surface, but still may have occasional gusts 30 to 40mph at \ntimes beginning early Thursday morning, persisting into Thursday\nevening. A Wind Advisory may be needed in future updates.\n\nAs the low continues to deepen and move north into New England\non Friday, wraparound showers and snow showers will be likely\nfor the favored western slopes. Light snowfall accumulations\npossible in western Greenbrier Friday night.\n\nCalmer weather returns for the weekend with temperatures warming\nback to above normal through early next week.",
